! Example ACL demonstrating all four anomaly types plus a clean baseline.
! Loosely modeled after the kind of edge-router ACL fwlint targets.

ip access-list extended EDGE-IN

! 10-20: collective shadowing -- rules 10 and 20 together cover all of
! 10.0.0.0/24's HTTPS traffic, so rule 30's DENY can never fire even though
! neither rule 10 nor rule 20 alone covers it.
 10 permit tcp 10.0.0.0 0.0.0.127 any eq 443
 20 permit tcp 10.0.0.128 0.0.0.127 any eq 443
 30 deny tcp 10.0.0.0 0.0.0.255 any eq 443

! 40-50: direct pairwise shadowing -- rule 50 can never fire, rule 40 already
! allows all of 10.0.1.0/24 regardless of protocol.
 40 permit ip 10.0.1.0 0.0.0.255 any
 50 deny tcp 10.0.1.0 0.0.0.255 any eq 22

! 60-70: redundancy -- rule 70 adds nothing; rule 60 already allows it.
 60 permit udp 10.0.2.0 0.0.0.255 any eq 53
 70 permit udp 10.0.2.0 0.0.0.255 any eq 53

! 80-90: generalization -- a specific deny exception followed by a broader
! allow default. Likely intentional, but flagged for review.
 80 deny tcp host 10.0.3.50 any eq 3389
 90 permit tcp 10.0.3.0 0.0.0.255 any eq 3389

! 100-110: correlation -- partial overlap, conflicting actions, neither
! rule contains the other. (Two CIDR-style address blocks can never
! partially overlap -- they're always nested or disjoint -- so this
! example overlaps in the port dimension instead: 8050-8100 is decided by
! whichever rule the operator intended, but the *other* rule's exclusive
! range still depends on order.)
100 permit tcp 10.0.4.0 0.0.0.255 any range 8000 8100
110 deny tcp 10.0.4.0 0.0.0.255 any range 8050 8150

! 120: unrelated clean rule, no anomaly.
120 permit tcp any host 198.51.100.10 eq 443

! implicit deny applies after this
